#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e106d50e-3d48-512d-b720-479cdc677d53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{9}(9(\\ln x)^{2} - 6\\ln (x) - 2) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065543} {:answer/id #uuid "22eb2b2d-4d67-5f08-b7aa-3ad8ff1c769f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{27}(18(\\ln x)^{2} - 3\\ln (x) - 1) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065544} {:answer/id #uuid "38a83f97-4767-5bd4-a9f2-dde96c9876b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{3}}{27}(9(\\ln x)^{2} - 6\\ln (x) + 2) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065545} {:answer/id #uuid "42b32e17-1e07-54e9-b6fd-b38de3ffad4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{3}}{9}(18(\\ln x)^{2} + 3\\ln (x) + 1) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065546} {:answer/id #uuid "460bef6c-3115-5fa6-93d9-3485bf22b303" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{3}}{27}(9(\\ln x)^{2} - 2\\ln (x) - 3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a83f97-4767-5bd4-a9f2-dde96c9876b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{3}}{27}(9(\\ln x)^{2} - 6\\ln (x) + 2) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065545} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaab2e22-4c0f-51ef-bc63-d3de1e7ab3a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065542}] :question/id #uuid "9435352d-2478-5c94-824b-ebf745d0d5a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-12706" :question/problem "What is $\\intx^{2}(\\ln x)^{2}dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669a636b-6611-50b8-84df-2a22afa2af9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669a636b-6611-50b8-84df-2a22afa2af9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302504} :answer-field/id #uuid "1747833e-00a1-5a08-8b51-d14818791771" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a32abdd-07d2-5cd6-a901-13295b56bc62" :question/math-academy-id "q-352011" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[(4{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =((4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+8(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+2+0-8)-0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb0e201-f1ed-5ed6-8c5b-b090543127b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340989} {:answer/id #uuid "c29a15bd-82df-5193-8952-3ea79f3b8217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c29a15bd-82df-5193-8952-3ea79f3b8217" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340990} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d19801-3829-53fc-8712-aa77403b3de1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2be1c0cd-adaf-53aa-b6b4-1aa499097229" :question/math-academy-id "q-352028" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=27{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=54x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(27{x}^{2})(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(54x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+18\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3})\\cdot 1\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+18\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+18(\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{9}+D) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}27{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-9{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =9{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}-2-2 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-4 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
